# The neutral descriptions. THE BET lives in this file.
#
# Each rule below is ONE authored description, and the engine reads it two ways:
#
#   forwards   structure  =>  description   -- RECOGNIZE intaken code
#   backwards  description =>  structure    -- the work order for WRITING it
#
# Neither reading is ours to build. That is survey §7, and it is the whole reason
# this generation exists: engine 2 deleted pattern matching, so the duality had to
# be reconstructed by reading a microfunction's body against its effects
# (`driver.establishes`). Here an antecedent IS what that reconstructed.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND THE TWO FILES BECAME ONE. Engine 2 needed `patterns.mf` to declare the
# neutral labels and `python.mf`'s BRIDGES to write them, because a microfunction
# is pointed one way and a description that could only be read could not also
# translate. Here the antecedent is Python's vocabulary and the consequent is the
# neutral one, so **the pattern and the bridge are one statement**. There is no
# second file to drift from.
#
# ⚠ THE AUTHORING CONSTRAINT THAT SURVIVED THE SUBSTRATE CHANGE UNTOUCHED: intake
# must not reuse a pattern's word. Intake says `for_stmt` / `iterated` / `body`;
# the description says `iteration` / `sequence` / `does`. If they coincided, the
# translation would do nothing for that part while looking like it worked, and the
# perturbation pin would not bite there. Coincidental agreement is exactly what
# this separation exists to make explicit.

# ⭐⭐ THE ABSTENTION IS AUTHORED, AND IT IS A MEMBER OF THE ANTECEDENT.
#
# `readable($x)` is asserted by intake on every node it read, and NOT on the
# placeholder it puts where an unmodelled construct was. So a description that
# names `+readable($s)` is saying: *I will not describe this loop's sequence if I
# could not read it.* Measured before it existed: `for x in [c for c in xs]` was
# recognized as an iteration and `sequence(loop, <unreadable>)` was asserted `+` —
# a CONFIDENTLY WRONG description, not a missed one, which is the exact shape
# engine 2 hit from the other end.
#
# ⭐ On engine 2 this judgement was Python, in `patterns.py`, and our own notes
# named it as the thing to move — *a judgement living where nothing can argue with
# it*. It is now a member, so a description declares its own refusals and another
# author can disagree by writing a different description.
#
# ⚠ It is POSITIVE on purpose. A rule cannot say *nothing claims this*: §9's `-`
# means *an entry denies this*, never *for no entry*. So intake asserts the good
# case and nobody fakes negation-as-failure.
#
# ⚠ `readable` is NOT `complete`. A block holding one unreadable statement is
# still a readable BLOCK, and `does($n, $b)` names the block — so the gap costs
# exactly the descriptions that bind the part it is in, which is engine 2's
# load-bearing rule arriving here as authoring rather than as machinery.

# ⚠⚠ EVERY RULE BELOW CARRIES `no <its own conclusion>` AS A PREMISE, AND A NEW RULE
# WITHOUT ONE IS A HANG. There is no inert set in this engine: an application that
# changed nothing is offered again, so a rule that does not stop itself never stops —
# and the symptom is not an error but a run that burns its whole budget on the FIRST
# applicable rule while every later rule never fires. See `mf.py`.

rule <iteration> = implies(
  { +for_stmt($n), +target($n, $v), +readable($v), +iterated($n, $s), +readable($s),
    +body($n, $b), +readable($b), no iteration($n) },
  { +iteration($n), +item($n, $v), +sequence($n, $s), +does($n, $b) } )

rule <conditional> = implies(
  { +if_stmt($n), +condition($n, $c), +readable($c), +then($n, $t), +readable($t),
    no choice($n) },
  { +choice($n), +tests($n, $c), +otherwise_does($n, $t) } )

rule <application> = implies(
  { +call($n), +callee($n, $f), +readable($f), no applies($n, $f) },
  { +applies($n, $f) } )
